summary:
- topic: Recognitions and Reports
  text: The meeting began with extensive recognitions. The Administration and Board
    honored music teacher Craig Uffington's induction into the Maine Music Educators
    Association Hall of Fame. Students were celebrated for achievements in music,
    robotics (receiving a $5,000 donation from Consolidated Communications), and athletics,
    with a special acknowledgment of the Boys Outdoor Track and Field team for winning
    the state championship.
- topic: Community Partnerships
  text: The Administration presented a comprehensive report on community partnerships,
    highlighting the contributions of nearly 500 volunteers, significant in-kind donations
    like food from the Locker Project and coats from Operation Warm, and the expansion
    of career exploration programs (ELOs) for students, which provide valuable real-world
    experience.
- topic: Federal Funding Update
  text: The Administration reported a significant decrease of nearly $150,000 in federal
    Title funds for the upcoming fiscal year. This reduction, primarily in Title I,
    was attributed to a lower state allocation and new guidance on funding for charter
    school students, which will impact planning for the FY26 budget.
- topic: Proposed Facilities Bond
  text: 'The Administration presented a potential bond referendum of up to $17 million
    to address major facility needs. The proposal includes three main components:
    enhancing security at all elementary schools with secure vestibules and cameras,
    addressing deferred maintenance like roofing and windows, and a complete renovation
    of the SPHS stadium complex.'
- topic: Athletic Field Debate
  text: A significant portion of the discussion focused on the stadium renovation.
    The Superintendent recommended a modern, fully recyclable artificial turf (Iron
    Turf with BrockFill infill) over natural grass, arguing it offers greater durability,
    accessibility for community use, and long-term resilience. The cost for the stadium
    portion is estimated between $10-13 million.
- topic: Board Environmental Concerns
  text: Several Board members raised strong concerns about the environmental impact
    of installing a plastic-based field, questioning the true recyclability of the
    product and whether it aligns with the city's 'One Climate Future' goals. They
    expressed skepticism about 'greenwashing' claims from manufacturers and the potential
    for the product to end up in a landfill.
- topic: Board Action on Bond Proposal
  text: "In response to the complex proposal and varied concerns, the Board voted\
    \ unanimously to direct the administration to prepare three separate bond referendum\
    \ proposals\u2014one for security, one for deferred maintenance, and one for the\
    \ athletic facilities. This allows the Board and the public to consider and vote\
    \ on each major initiative independently."
